---
import BaseLayout from "../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import PromptPage from "../../../components/PromptPage.astro";

const masterPrompt = `Act as an expert customer researcher, market researcher, buyer-insight analyst and marketing strategist.

TASK:
Identify and structure the most important customer pain points for the supplied market, audience, product or service.

The goal is not to guess randomly.

Separate:
- known evidence
- reasonable inference
- assumptions
- unknowns that require research

Use the findings to help improve:
- marketing messages
- product positioning
- campaign ideas
- content topics
- sales conversations
- offers
- customer experience

BUSINESS / BRAND:
[Name and description.]

PRODUCT OR SERVICE:
[What is being sold or offered?]

TARGET CUSTOMER:
[Describe the customer.]

MARKET / LOCATION:
[Country / region / industry / online/global.]

CUSTOMER ROLE:
[Owner / manager / employee / parent / student / creator / consumer / other.]

USE CASE:
[What are they trying to accomplish?]

PURCHASE CONTEXT:
[Why or when might they need this product or service?]

CURRENT ALTERNATIVES:
[What do they currently use instead?]

KNOWN CUSTOMER FEEDBACK:
[Paste reviews, comments, interview notes, support messages or other evidence.]

KNOWN PROBLEMS:
[List anything already known.]

KNOWN OBJECTIONS:
[List anything already known.]

PRICE RANGE:
[If relevant.]

COMPETITORS:
[Optional.]

RESEARCH SOURCES AVAILABLE:
[Reviews / forums / Reddit / surveys / interviews / CRM / support tickets / search data / none.]

RESEARCH GOAL:
[Messaging / product improvement / campaign / positioning / content / sales / other.]

SPECIAL REQUIREMENTS:
[Any additional instructions.]

CUSTOMER PAIN-POINT RESEARCH REQUIREMENTS:

1. DEFINE THE CUSTOMER CONTEXT FIRST
Understand:
Who they are
What they are trying to do
What situation creates the need
What success looks like

2. DO NOT INVENT CUSTOMER PROBLEMS
If evidence is missing, label the issue as:
Hypothesis
Possible pain point
Research question

3. SEPARATE EVIDENCE FROM ASSUMPTION

4. CLASSIFY EVIDENCE STRENGTH
Use:
Strong
Moderate
Weak
Unknown

5. IDENTIFY FUNCTIONAL PAIN POINTS
Examples:
Too slow
Too expensive
Too difficult
Too manual
Too unreliable
Too complex
Too limited

6. IDENTIFY EMOTIONAL PAIN POINTS
Examples:
Stress
Fear
Frustration
Confusion
Embarrassment
Lack of confidence
Overwhelm

7. IDENTIFY SOCIAL PAIN POINTS
Examples:
Fear of looking unprofessional
Pressure from management
Customer expectations
Peer comparison
Reputation risk

8. IDENTIFY FINANCIAL PAIN POINTS
Examples:
Waste
Unexpected cost
Cash-flow pressure
Overstock
Lost sales
High operating cost

9. IDENTIFY TIME PAIN POINTS
Examples:
Repeated manual work
Waiting
Delays
Slow approval
Slow learning curve

10. IDENTIFY RISK PAIN POINTS
Examples:
Errors
Compliance
Safety
Stockouts
Data loss
Poor decisions
Customer complaints

11. IDENTIFY KNOWLEDGE PAIN POINTS
Examples:
Not knowing what to do
Too much conflicting information
No clear process
Lack of expertise

12. IDENTIFY WORKFLOW PAIN POINTS
Look for friction between:
People
Systems
Tools
Departments
Steps
Approvals

13. IDENTIFY TRIGGER EVENTS
What causes the customer to actively search for a solution?

14. POSSIBLE TRIGGERS:
Failure
Deadline
New responsibility
Growth
Cost increase
Customer complaint
Staff shortage
Audit
Technology change
New business launch

15. IDENTIFY FREQUENCY
Estimate whether each problem occurs:
Daily
Weekly
Monthly
Occasionally
Rarely

16. LABEL ESTIMATED FREQUENCY AS AN ASSUMPTION WHEN UNSUPPORTED

17. IDENTIFY PAIN SEVERITY
Use:
Low
Medium
High
Critical

18. EXPLAIN WHY SEVERITY MATTERS

19. IDENTIFY BUSINESS OR PERSONAL CONSEQUENCE
What happens if the problem remains unresolved?

20. IDENTIFY DESIRED OUTCOME
Turn each pain point into the outcome the customer actually wants.

21. EXAMPLE:
Pain: Stock records do not match physical inventory.
Desired outcome: Reliable inventory records and faster discrepancy resolution.

22. IDENTIFY JOB-TO-BE-DONE
Express what the customer is trying to achieve.

23. USE THIS FORMAT WHEN HELPFUL:
"When [situation], I want to [motivation], so I can [desired outcome]."

24. IDENTIFY CURRENT WORKAROUNDS
What does the customer do today?

25. WORKAROUNDS MAY INCLUDE:
Spreadsheets
Manual checks
WhatsApp
Email
Paper
Multiple apps
Outsourcing
Ignoring the problem
Building an internal process

26. IDENTIFY WHY CURRENT ALTERNATIVES FAIL

27. DO NOT ASSUME THE EXISTING SOLUTION IS COMPLETELY BAD
Customers may tolerate weaknesses because of:
Price
Habit
Integration
Trust
Switching cost
Training effort

28. IDENTIFY SWITCHING BARRIERS

29. POSSIBLE BARRIERS:
Price
Learning curve
Migration
Approval
Trust
Time
Data transfer
Contract
Habit

30. IDENTIFY PURCHASE OBJECTIONS

31. DISTINGUISH PAIN FROM OBJECTION
Pain = problem before purchase.
Objection = reason not to choose the proposed solution.

32. COMMON OBJECTIONS:
Too expensive
Not sure it works
Too complicated
No time to implement
Already have a solution
Need management approval
Security concerns
Not enough proof

33. IDENTIFY TRUST REQUIREMENTS
What does the customer need to believe before taking action?

34. POSSIBLE TRUST SIGNALS:
Demonstration
Case study
Transparent pricing
Free trial
Credentials
Reviews
Comparison
Guarantee
Documentation
Human support

35. DO NOT INVENT TRUST SIGNALS THAT THE BUSINESS DOES NOT HAVE

36. IDENTIFY CUSTOMER LANGUAGE
Capture how customers naturally describe the problem.

37. AVOID REPLACING CUSTOMER LANGUAGE WITH CORPORATE JARGON

38. BUILD A VOCABULARY LIST
Include:
Problem phrases
Desired-outcome phrases
Objection phrases
Search phrases

39. DO NOT FABRICATE DIRECT QUOTES
Only present quotations when actual source text is supplied.

40. PARAPHRASE UNSOURCED LANGUAGE AS HYPOTHETICAL

41. IDENTIFY SEARCH INTENT
What might the customer search when experiencing the problem?

42. CLASSIFY SEARCH INTENT:
Problem-aware
Solution-aware
Product-aware
Comparison
Purchase-ready

43. CREATE SEARCH-STYLE PHRASES
Do not claim actual search volume unless data is supplied.

44. IDENTIFY INFORMATION GAPS
What important questions remain unanswered?

45. PRIORITIZE RESEARCH GAPS
Which unknowns would most change the marketing decision?

46. CREATE CUSTOMER INTERVIEW QUESTIONS

47. INTERVIEW QUESTIONS SHOULD BE OPEN-ENDED

48. AVOID LEADING QUESTIONS

49. ASK ABOUT RECENT REAL EVENTS
Example:
"Tell me about the last time this happened."

50. ASK WHAT THEY DID NEXT

51. ASK WHAT THE CONSEQUENCE WAS

52. ASK WHAT THEY HAVE ALREADY TRIED

53. ASK WHY THEY CHOSE THAT APPROACH

54. ASK WHAT WAS FRUSTRATING

55. ASK WHAT AN IDEAL SOLUTION WOULD CHANGE

56. ASK WHAT WOULD PREVENT THEM FROM USING A NEW SOLUTION

57. CREATE SURVEY QUESTIONS WHEN APPROPRIATE

58. DO NOT RELY ONLY ON SURVEYS FOR DEEP MOTIVATION

59. IDENTIFY RESEARCH CHANNELS
Possible:
Customer interviews
Sales calls
Support tickets
Product reviews
Competitor reviews
Forums
Reddit
Facebook groups
Search suggestions
Community discussions
CRM notes

60. PRIORITIZE FIRST-PARTY EVIDENCE WHEN AVAILABLE

61. DISTINGUISH COMPETITOR PAIN FROM MARKET PAIN
A complaint about one competitor may not apply to the whole market.

62. LOOK FOR REPEATED THEMES

63. DO NOT OVERWEIGHT ONE EXTREME REVIEW

64. LOOK FOR CONTRADICTIONS
Different customer segments may experience different problems.

65. SEGMENT WHEN NECESSARY

66. POSSIBLE SEGMENTS:
Beginner vs expert
Small vs large company
Low-budget vs premium
Manager vs user
New customer vs existing customer
Individual vs team

67. DO NOT FORCE ALL CUSTOMERS INTO ONE PERSONA

68. IDENTIFY HIGH-VALUE PAIN POINTS
Prioritize problems that are:
Frequent
Severe
Expensive
Urgent
Poorly solved

69. CREATE A PRIORITY SCORE
Use qualitative scoring unless reliable numbers are available.

70. POSSIBLE PRIORITY FACTORS:
Severity
Frequency
Urgency
Commercial value
Solution fit
Evidence strength

71. DO NOT CREATE FALSE NUMERICAL PRECISION

72. USE HIGH / MEDIUM / LOW WHEN DATA IS LIMITED

73. IDENTIFY MESSAGE OPPORTUNITIES
For each high-priority pain point, suggest a credible message angle.

74. MESSAGE SHOULD CONNECT:
Pain
Desired outcome
Real solution capability

75. DO NOT PROMISE MORE THAN THE PRODUCT CAN DELIVER

76. IDENTIFY CONTENT OPPORTUNITIES
Examples:
How-to
Checklist
Comparison
Mistakes
Troubleshooting
FAQ
Guide
Calculator
Template
Case example

77. IDENTIFY CAMPAIGN ANGLES
Examples:
Problem recognition
Education
Demonstration
Risk reduction
Convenience
Time saving
Cost control

78. IDENTIFY PRODUCT OPPORTUNITIES
Where appropriate, highlight:
Missing features
Usability improvements
Support needs
Onboarding gaps

79. DO NOT ASSUME EVERY CUSTOMER COMPLAINT REQUIRES A PRODUCT FEATURE

80. IDENTIFY SALES-ENABLEMENT OPPORTUNITIES
Examples:
Objection-handling sheet
FAQ
Demo scenario
ROI discussion
Comparison guide

81. CREATE A PAIN-TO-MESSAGE MAP

82. FOR EACH IMPORTANT PAIN INCLUDE:
Pain
Desired outcome
Evidence
Message angle
Proof required
CTA idea

83. CREATE A PAIN-TO-CONTENT MAP

84. CREATE A PAIN-TO-PRODUCT MAP WHEN RELEVANT

85. IDENTIFY CLAIM RISKS
Flag statements that require evidence.

86. EXAMPLES OF CLAIM RISKS:
Saves 50% time
Cuts costs
Eliminates errors
Best
Fastest
Guaranteed

87. DO NOT PRESENT ASSUMPTIONS AS FINDINGS

88. USE CONFIDENCE LABELS

89. EXAMPLE:
High confidence — repeated in supplied customer feedback.
Medium confidence — consistent with multiple indirect signals.
Low confidence — plausible but currently unverified.

90. CREATE A VALIDATION PLAN
Explain how to test the most important assumptions.

91. PRIORITIZE LOW-COST VALIDATION

92. EXAMPLES:
5 customer interviews
Review 50 support tickets
Analyse competitor reviews
Test two landing-page messages
Run a small survey

93. DO NOT RECOMMEND LARGE RESEARCH PROJECTS WHEN A SMALL TEST CAN ANSWER THE QUESTION

94. IDENTIFY WHAT WOULD DISPROVE THE ASSUMPTION

95. INCLUDE NEGATIVE EVIDENCE
If evidence suggests a supposed pain point is not important, say so.

96. AVOID CONFIRMATION BIAS

97. STATE LIMITATIONS
Explain where conclusions are uncertain.

98. CREATE NEXT RESEARCH ACTIONS

99. RANK THEM BY DECISION VALUE

100. FINAL QUALITY CHECK
Before finalizing verify:

- customer context is clear
- evidence and assumptions are separated
- functional pain points are considered
- emotional pain points are considered
- financial pain points are considered
- time pain points are considered
- risk pain points are considered
- triggers are identified
- consequences are identified
- desired outcomes are identified
- workarounds are identified
- objections are separated from pains
- switching barriers are considered
- customer language is practical
- no direct quotes were fabricated
- search phrases are not presented as volume data
- segments are considered where relevant
- pain points are prioritized
- evidence strength is stated
- message opportunities are realistic
- product claims are not exaggerated
- unknowns are identified
- validation actions are practical
- limitations are clearly stated

OUTPUT FORMAT:

1. CUSTOMER CONTEXT
Summarize:
Customer
Situation
Job to be done
Desired outcome
Current alternatives

2. PAIN-POINT MAP

For each pain point provide:
Pain point
Category
Trigger
Consequence
Desired outcome
Frequency
Severity
Evidence strength
Confidence
Current workaround

3. TOP PRIORITY PAIN POINTS
Rank the most important problems and explain why.

4. CUSTOMER LANGUAGE
Provide:
Problem phrases
Desired-outcome phrases
Objection phrases
Likely search-style phrases

Clearly label unsourced wording as hypothetical.

5. OBJECTIONS & SWITCHING BARRIERS
Separate:
Purchase objections
Trust concerns
Switching barriers

6. PAIN-TO-MESSAGE MAP
For each priority pain provide:
Pain
Message angle
Real benefit
Proof needed
CTA

7. CONTENT OPPORTUNITIES
Suggest practical content based on the research.

8. CAMPAIGN OPPORTUNITIES
Suggest campaign angles based on the strongest pain points.

9. PRODUCT / SERVICE OPPORTUNITIES
Highlight improvements worth investigating.

10. RESEARCH GAPS
List what is still unknown.

11. VALIDATION PLAN
Give the lowest-cost practical way to validate the most important assumptions.

12. CUSTOMER INTERVIEW QUESTIONS
Provide concise open-ended questions.

13. CLAIM-RISK CHECK
Identify statements that should not be used without evidence.

14. FINAL INSIGHT SUMMARY
Explain:
What appears most important
What is well supported
What remains uncertain
What should be researched next

IMPORTANT:
- Do not invent customer evidence.
- Distinguish facts, inference and hypotheses.
- Do not fabricate quotes.
- Do not fabricate search volume.
- Prioritize recurring, severe and commercially relevant problems.
- Use customer language rather than unnecessary jargon.
- Recommend validation when evidence is weak.
- Keep marketing conclusions proportional to the available evidence.`;

const steps = [
  {
    title: "Start with real customer context",
    description:
      "Define what the customer is trying to accomplish, what triggers the problem and what they currently do before listing possible frustrations.",
  },
  {
    title: "Separate evidence from assumptions",
    description:
      "Label whether each pain point comes from actual customer feedback, indirect evidence or a hypothesis that still needs validation.",
  },
  {
    title: "Prioritize the problems that matter",
    description:
      "Focus on pains that combine severity, frequency, urgency, strong solution fit and credible evidence instead of treating every complaint equally.",
  },
  {
    title: "Turn research into action",
    description:
      "Connect high-priority pain points to messaging, content, campaign angles and low-cost validation steps.",
  },
];

const example = {
  input: [
    "Audience: Small warehouse and e-commerce operators.",
    "Problem area: Inventory accuracy.",
    "Research goal: Improve marketing messages for an inventory discrepancy tool.",
    "Known issue: Physical quantity sometimes differs from system quantity.",
    "Available evidence: Informal customer conversations and common operational experience.",
  ],

  output: [
    "HIGH-PRIORITY PAIN: Staff discover stock differences but do not have a consistent investigation process. Evidence strength should remain limited until direct customer research confirms frequency.",
    "CONSEQUENCE: Time is spent checking receiving, picking, adjustments and transaction history while customer orders or replenishment decisions may be delayed.",
    "DESIRED OUTCOME: A clear repeatable method for identifying where the discrepancy occurred and documenting corrective action.",
    "MESSAGE ANGLE: 'Physical stock doesn't match the system? Check the likely causes in a structured order.'",
    "CONTENT OPPORTUNITY: Create a practical '6 areas to check first' guide rather than claiming the tool eliminates inventory errors.",
    "VALIDATION: Interview several warehouse supervisors and ask about the most recent discrepancy they investigated, what they checked first and where the process became difficult.",
  ],
};

const tips = [
  {
    title: "A plausible problem is not yet evidence",
    description:
      "Industry experience can generate strong hypotheses, but customer interviews, support data, reviews and real behaviour should determine whether the problem is genuinely important.",
  },
  {
    title: "Ask about the last real incident",
    description:
      "Questions about a recent actual event usually produce more useful information than asking customers what they think they might do in a hypothetical situation.",
  },
  {
    title: "Pain points should lead somewhere",
    description:
      "The most useful research connects customer frustration to a desired outcome, credible message, proof requirement and practical next action.",
  },
];

---

<BaseLayout
  title="Customer Pain Point Research Prompt | KRIYANO"
  description="Research and prioritize customer pain points, triggers, desired outcomes, objections, workarounds and marketing opportunities while separating evidence from assumptions."
>
  <PromptPage
    category="MARKETING"
    title="Customer Pain Point Research"
    accentTitle="Prompt."
    description="Identify and prioritize customer frustrations, triggers, desired outcomes, objections and buying language while clearly separating evidence from assumptions."
    meta={[
      "Free prompt",
      "Customer research",
      "Marketing insight",
    ]}
    bestFor={[
      "Marketing strategy",
      "Campaign planning",
      "Customer interviews",
      "Positioning",
      "Content ideas",
      "Product research",
    ]}
    masterPrompt={masterPrompt}
    introTitle="Find the problem before writing the message."
    introDescription="Map what customers are trying to achieve, where they struggle, what they already use and which pain points have enough evidence to influence your marketing."
    steps={steps}
    exampleTitle="Research inventory-accuracy pain points without inventing evidence."
    example={example}
    tipsTitle="Strong marketing insight needs evidence, not just plausible assumptions."
    tips={tips}
    sidebarTitle="Ready to build the value proposition?"
    sidebarDescription="Use KRIYANO's Value Proposition Builder next to turn validated customer pains and desired outcomes into clearer positioning and messaging."
    warningTitle="Customer insight can sound more certain than the evidence supports"
    warningText="Treat AI-generated pain points as research hypotheses unless they are supported by real customer feedback, behavioural data, reviews, interviews or other reliable evidence."
  />
</BaseLayout>